{"agent_id":"pp3g-GGEY-read-only-design-proposal-task-attempt-2-for-issue","agent_seq":7,"timestamp":"2026-08-10T02:37:44.203918636Z","event":{"type":"CommentAdded","issue_uuid":"8cda3348-0d9e-4809-b7da-afcddda27804","comment_uuid":"042038d9-504e-4305-8288-cc588b8874c7","author":"pp3g-GGEY-read-only-design-proposal-task-attempt-2-for-issue","content":"# Deny-by-Default Capability Boundary for Read-Only Roles — Proposal (#342)\n\n**Status**: Proposal for operator approval. No config was modified (read-only design task).\n**Scope**: ASES read-only roles (reviewer, auditor). Deliverable answers the unfinished half of #340: the broad `opencode *`, `git *`, `cargo *`, `npm *` grants that overstate \"read-only by construction\" (8/8 reviewer consensus, #314/#320/#326).\n**Precedent**: This proposal extends the #340 crosslink-allowlist narrow-allowlist approach to the remaining grants, and starts from the #342 auditor's independent bypass matrix (02:00 comment) as the completeness checklist.\n\n---\n\n## 0. Verification summary (what this proposal is built on)\n\nSource-verified, not re-derived:\n\n1. **The `--pure` mechanism is CONFIRMED** — `fork/opencode-src/packages/opencode/src/plugin/index.ts:179`: `const plugins = flags.pure ? [] : (cfg.plugin_origins ?? [])`. The three guard plugins are loaded from `plugin_origins`, which is derived from the config `plugin` array (`.opencode/opencode.json` → `plugin` → `plugin_origins`, see `src/config/config.ts:344-348`). Therefore `opencode run --pure` empties all three guards.\n2. **`internalPlugins()` (index.ts:66) is a separate built-in set** — auth-only: CodexAuthPlugin, CopilotAuthPlugin, ModalPlugin, GitlabAuthPlugin, PoeAuthPlugin, CloudflareWorkersAuthPlugin, CloudflareAIGatewayAuthPlugin, AzureAuthPlugin, DigitalOceanAuthPlugin, SnowflakeCortexAuthPlugin, XaiAuthPlugin. **None of the three guards is in it.** They survive `--pure` only because they are internal; the guards do not.\n3. **Two-layer enforcement mapped**:\n   - **Layer 1 — opencode permission block** (`.opencode/agents/{reviewer,auditor}.md`, `permission.bash`): deny-by-default (`\"*\": deny` + prefix allows). **This is the load-bearing read-only boundary.**\n   - **Layer 2 — crosslink-guard plugin** (`.crosslink/hook-config.json`): `blocked_git` per-type lists + `allowed_bash_prefixes` pass-through + active-issue gate. **This layer is NOT a deny-by-default bash boundary.** For agent contexts the effective `tracking_mode` is `relaxed` (`agent_overrides.tracking_mode`), and crosslink-guard.ts section 7 allows any non-blocked-git command in relaxed mode; section 9 also short-circuits to ALLOW whenever an active-issue sentinel is present. The `allowed_bash_prefixes` list is a pass-through fast-path, not an allowlist gate.\n   - **Consequence**: for reviewer/auditor, the plugin layer blocks only the enumerated git mutators (section 3). Everything else — cargo, npm, npx, node, python, rtk proxies, gh — is plugin-ALLOWED and depends entirely on Layer 1. Any proposal that only edits `hook-config.json` would change nothing for read-only roles; **the fix must land in the agent permission blocks.**\n\n---\n\n## 1. Bypass matrix (auditor's matrix, verified + extended)\n\n| Grant | Current surface | What it enables today | Risk | Verdict |\n|---|---|---|---|---|\n| `opencode *` (both) | run/tui/serve/agent subcommands | `opencode run --pure` disables all 3 guard plugins (verified §0.1); spawned process self-selects any agent (e.g. `--agent builder`) with full bash and no guard plugins; `opencode run --auto` auto-approves permissions; direct invocation bypasses the claude wrapper's model enforcement | **HIGH** — single-command escape from the entire enforcement layer | **REMOVE** |\n| `git *` (both) | all git | Read-only evidence commands (status/diff/log/show/branch) PLUS unlisted mutators not in `blocked_git`: `git config core.hooksPath`, `git update-ref`, `git checkout <branch>` / `-- <path>`, `git switch`, `git restore <path>` (only `restore .` blocked), `git rm`, `git mv`, `git branch <name>` (create; only `-d/-D/-m` blocked), `git stash create` (bare `stash` blocked), `git remote add`, `git submodule add`, `git notes add`, `git replace`, `git filter-branch`, `git fetch origin main:main` (writes refs), `git pull` (fetch+merge), `git worktree add` | **MEDIUM-HIGH** — blocklist whack-a-mole; every new mutator needs a new list entry | **NARROW** to explicit read-only prefixes |\n| `cargo *` (reviewer only) | build/test/run/check/clippy/fmt/install | `cargo run`/`cargo test` execute project code; `cargo build` executes `build.rs` (arbitrary code exec at compile time); `cargo install` writes binaries. Also pass-through-allowed in hook-config `allowed_bash_prefixes` (`cargo test/build/check/clippy/fmt`) | **HIGH** — arbitrary code execution | **REMOVE** |\n| `npm *` (reviewer only) | test/run/install/exec | `npm run` executes package.json scripts; `npx <pkg>` downloads + executes arbitrary packages; `npm install` writes node_modules + lifecycle scripts. Also pass-through-allowed in hook-config (`npm test/run`, `npx `) | **HIGH** — arbitrary code execution | **REMOVE** |\n| crosslink narrow list (both) | issue comment/show, session status, knowledge search/list/add/edit, sync | Legitimate store-access surface (tripn finding #440/#445); lifecycle/dispatch (quick, issue create/close, session work, kickoff, swarm, intervene) NOT granted | **OK** — this is the intended durable-position surface | **KEEP**; fix `crosslink sync` literal-pattern bug |\n| `ls *`, `cat *` (both) | file read | None — read-only. Redundant with native read/glob/grep tools but harmless and useful for shell-based evidence | **OK** | **KEEP** |\n| `rtk *` (both) | proxy surface, NOT read-only-only | Auditor matrix listed rtk as \"OK — read-only file operations\", but the rtk CLI exposes a **broad proxy surface**: `rtk git` (proxies git mutators — caught by plugin normalization at crosslink-guard.ts:138 only for the git blocklist), `rtk gh`/`rtk glab` (GitHub/GitLab CLI), `rtk docker`, `rtk kubectl`, `rtk pnpm`, `rtk dotnet`, `rtk aws`, `rtk psql`, `rtk wget`, `rtk test`/`jest`/`vitest`/`tsc`/`next`/`lint` (run builds/tests), `rtk summary`/`err`/`smart` (RUN arbitrary commands then filter output), `rtk config`/`init` (writes config) | **MEDIUM** — understated by the auditor; the `rtk *` grant is not read-only by construction | **NARROW** to read-only proxies |\n\n**Completeness check against the auditor's checklist**: opencode * ✅, git * ✅, cargo * ✅, npm * ✅, crosslink * ✅, ls ✅, cat ✅, rtk * ✅ — all eight covered, with `rtk *` extended and the two-layer enforcement finding added.\n\n---\n\n## 2. KEEP / NARROW / REMOVE — exact per-grant decision\n\n### 2.1 `opencode *` — REMOVE (both roles)\n\n```yaml\n# reviewer.md / auditor.md — REMOVE this line:\n\"opencode *\": allow\n```\n\n**Rationale**: the `--pure` escape is a single-command exit from the only layer that carries the read-only boundary (Layer 1 + guard plugins). No review/audit workflow step was found that requires a read-only role to spawn an opencode process — read-only roles are themselves opencode subagent sessions; they do not launch sessions.\n\n**Optional narrow exception** (decision point): `\"opencode models *\": \"allow\"` — the model-catalog query is read-only and supports model-discipline verification in-role (the orchestrator already carries this grant). If the harness wants read-only roles to verify model IDs themselves, add exactly this one prefix. Otherwise remove opencode entirely.\n\n**Tradeoff stated**: any session-capable opencode subcommand (`run`, `serve`, `tui`, `agent`, `auth`) is an escape vector — there is **no safe middle ground** between removing them and keeping the escape. `opencode models` is the only demonstrably harmless subcommand. If the harness believes read-only roles MUST spawn opencode sessions, that belief is itself a boundary violation and should be re-examined at dispatch level (the orchestrator launches opencode; read-only roles never should).\n\n### 2.2 `git *` — NARROW to explicit read-only prefixes (both roles)\n\n```yaml\n# reviewer.md / auditor.md — REPLACE \"git *\": \"allow\" with:\n\"git status *\": allow\n\"git diff *\": allow\n\"git log *\": allow\n\"git show *\": allow\n\"git branch -a *\": allow\n\"git branch -r *\": allow\n\"git branch -vv *\": allow\n\"git branch --show-current *\": allow\n```\n\n**What this keeps**: the evidence surface the tripn finding and the reviewer workflow (`reviewer.md` line 75: \"Read all changed files (use `git diff` or read files directly)\") confirm is genuinely needed — `git diff`/`git log`/`git show` for evidence verification, `git status` for state, `git branch` in listing-only forms (`-a`, `-r`, `-vv`, `--show-current`, `--list`).\n\n**What this structurally eliminates** (deny-by-default makes the whole blocklist moot): config, update-ref, checkout, switch, restore (any path), rm, mv, branch creation, stash (incl. `stash create`), tag, remote, submodule, notes, replace, filter-branch, worktree add, pull, fetch (general), push, merge, rebase, commit, am, apply, cherry-pick, reset, clean.\n\n**Optional addition** (decision point): `\"git fetch . *\": \"allow\"` — tripn residual gap E (\"read-only git (`git show/log/fetch .` only) for branch-diff verification\"). `git fetch .` reads from the shared local object store and lets an in-sandbox reviewer inspect a sibling worktree's branch without leaving the sandbox. **Tradeoff**: fetch does write to `.git` (FETCH_HEAD, refs/remotes) — it mutates repository metadata but never the worktree, and `git fetch .` never touches the network. Include only if branch-diff-in-sandbox evidence is a real need; otherwise omit and keep the orchestrator-relay path.\n\n**Explicitly excluded**: `git -C <path> ...` — the arg-after-`-C` makes the subcommand unscopable by prefix matcher, and the blocked-git normalization only rescues the enumerated mutators. Read-only roles operate in their own worktree; drop `git -C`.\n\n### 2.3 `cargo *` — REMOVE (reviewer only)\n\n```yaml\n# reviewer.md — REMOVE this line:\n\"cargo *\": \"allow\"\n```\n\n**Rationale**: reviewers do not build. Build/test evidence is produced by the builder and posted to the issue; the reviewer's job is to read the diff and the posted evidence, not to re-execute it. `cargo run`/`test`/`build` (build.rs) are arbitrary-code-execution surfaces. Removing them aligns the role with \"read-only by construction\".\n\n### 2.4 `npm *` — REMOVE (reviewer only)\n\n```yaml\n# reviewer.md — REMOVE this line:\n\"npm *\": \"allow\"\n```\n\n**Rationale**: same as cargo — `npm run`/`npx`/`install` execute arbitrary project or downloaded code. Reviewer does not install or run.\n\n### 2.5 crosslink narrow allowlist — KEEP (both roles), fix one bug\n\n```yaml\n# reviewer.md / auditor.md — KEEP all of these (unchanged from #340):\n\"crosslink issue comment *\": allow\n\"crosslink issue show *\": allow\n\"crosslink session status *\": allow\n\"crosslink knowledge search *\": allow\n\"crosslink knowledge list *\": allow\n\"crosslink knowledge add *\": allow\n\"crosslink knowledge edit *\": allow\n# auditor.md ALSO keeps:\n\"crosslink sync\": allow\n```\n\n**Bug fix** (auditor finding, 02:00): the literal pattern `\"crosslink sync\": allow` fails to match in permission enforcement. Change to `\"crosslink sync *\": allow` (trailing wildcard, matching the convention of every other pattern in the block). Verify at implementation time.\n\n**Drift note**: `reviewer.md` currently also carries `\"crosslink sync\": allow` (line 12), though the canonical tripn record (§2.2) designed sync as auditor-only. The current checkpoint contract (§5.4 playbook) requires reviewers to sync after posting positions, so sync-for-reviewer is justified today — but it is drift from the record and should be consciously re-confirmed or reverted. Recommendation: **keep for both** (checkpoint contract requires it), and update the tripn record when implemented.\n\n**Explicitly not granted** (unchanged): quick, issue create/close, session work, kickoff, swarm, intervene, issue review, knowledge delete.\n\n### 2.6 `ls *`, `cat *` — KEEP (both roles)\n\nRead-only; redundant with native read/glob/grep tools but zero security delta and convenient for shell-based evidence. Removing gains nothing; keeping loses nothing.\n\n### 2.7 `rtk *` — NARROW to read-only proxies (both roles)\n\n```yaml\n# reviewer.md / auditor.md — REPLACE \"rtk *\": \"allow\" with the read-only core:\n\"rtk read *\": allow\n\"rtk ls *\": allow\n\"rtk tree *\": allow\n\"rtk grep *\": allow\n\"rtk find *\": allow\n\"rtk wc *\": allow\n\"rtk diff *\": allow\n```\n\n**Rationale**: the auditor's matrix understated rtk as \"read-only file operations\". The rtk CLI (verified via `rtk --help`) proxies gh/glab/docker/kubectl/pnpm/dotnet/aws/psql/wget, runs tests/builds (test/jest/vitest/tsc/next/lint), and **runs arbitrary commands** (summary/err/smart) — none of which belongs in a read-only role. The seven prefixes above are the genuinely read-only, token-optimized file-inspection proxies. Everything else (`rtk git`, `rtk gh`, `rtk docker`, `rtk wget`, `rtk summary`, `rtk config`, ...) falls to `\"*\": deny`.\n\n**Tradeoff**: minor — reviewers already have read/glob/grep; rtk's remaining value is token-optimized output for large files. `rtk read` preserves that.\n\n### 2.8 Second surface — hook-config `allowed_bash_prefixes` (defense-in-depth; NOT the primary fix)\n\nThe global list in `.crosslink/hook-config.json` currently pass-throughs code-execution prefixes for **every** agent type: `\"opencode \"`, `\"cargo test/build/check/clippy/fmt\"`, `\"npm test\"`, `\"npm run\"`, `\"npx \"`, `\"tsc\"`, `\"node \"`, `\"python \"`. After §2.1–§2.4, Layer 1 denies these for reviewer/auditor, so they are dormant for read-only roles today — but the list is misleading as a boundary and would leak to any future role whose Layer-1 block allows them.\n\n**Recommendation**:\n- REMOVE from the global list: `opencode `, cargo test/build/check/clippy/fmt, npm test/run, npx, tsc, node, python.\n- If builders need them (they have `bash: allow` at Layer 1 anyway, so the plugin list is only a pass-through convenience), MOVE them to a `by_type.builder.allowed_bash_prefixes` override.\n- KEEP in the global list: `crosslink `, read-only git prefixes, ls/cat/rtk read, ps/pgrep/tmux read-only, grep/tail/head/wc/stat/diff/free/df/uptime/date/which/env, git worktree list.\n\nThis is a hardening step, not the boundary — the boundary is Layer 1 (§0.3).\n\n---\n\n## 3. The `--pure` mechanism and the fix\n\n**Mechanism (verified, one grep)**:\n- `.opencode/opencode.json` `plugin: [\"./plugins/orchestrator-guard.ts\", \"./plugins/crosslink-guard.ts\", \"./plugins/rtk-guard.ts\"]` — all three guards are **config plugins**, hence `plugin_origins`.\n- `fork/opencode-src/packages/opencode/src/plugin/index.ts:179`: `const plugins = flags.pure ? [] : (cfg.plugin_origins ?? [])` — `--pure` empties `plugin_origins`.\n- `index.ts:66-84` `internalPlugins()` — auth plugins only (listed §0.2). **No guard is internal.** `--pure` does not disable them, but that is irrelevant: the guards are not in that set.\n- Config resolution (`src/config/config.ts:344-348`) maps the `plugin` array to `plugin_origins` — confirming the three guards live in the array `--pure` empties.\n\n**Escape chain**: a read-only agent with `opencode *` runs `opencode run --pure --agent builder \"<prompt>\"` (plus `--auto` for approvals). The spawned process loads the same `opencode.json` but with `plugin_origins` emptied — **no crosslink-guard, no orchestrator-guard, no rtk-guard** — and `--agent builder` selects an agent whose permission block grants full bash and edit. The parent session's deny-by-default block does not govern the child process. The read-only boundary evaporates in one command.\n\n**The fix**: remove `opencode *` from the read-only permission blocks (§2.1). No opencode source change is required and none is proposed — the boundary is correctly placed at the permission layer; the grant was simply too wide. The `--pure` flag itself is a legitimate opencode feature; the defect is that a role that must never spawn sessions was granted the ability to.\n\n---\n\n## 4. Tradeoffs\n\n| Change | What read-only roles lose | Why acceptable |\n|---|---|---|\n| REMOVE `opencode *` | No in-role opencode invocation; no in-role `opencode models` unless the narrow exception is kept | Read-only roles are opencode subagent sessions; they do not launch sessions. Model-catalog verification remains the orchestrator's job (it has `opencode models *`). The only cost is if the harness expected in-role catalog checks — add the one prefix if so |\n| NARROW `git *` | `git fetch .` branch-diff-in-sandbox (unless opted in), `git -C` cross-worktree inspection, all mutators (correctly) | Reviewers keep diff/log/show/status — the evidence surface confirmed by tripn + reviewer.md. Cross-worktree/branch evidence falls back to the existing orchestrator-relay path or the optional `git fetch .` prefix |\n| REMOVE `cargo *`, `npm *` | No in-role build/test execution | Role separation: builders produce test evidence and post it; reviewers read evidence. Re-executing project code in a read-only role is precisely the capability the 8-reviewer consensus flagged |\n| NARROW `rtk *` | rtk gh/docker/kubectl/summary/etc. proxies | None of these belongs in read-only roles; the seven kept prefixes preserve token-optimized file inspection |\n| KEEP `ls *`/`cat *` | nothing | Redundant-but-harmless; zero security delta |\n| Fix `crosslink sync` pattern + hook-config cleanup | nothing functional | Bug fix + defense-in-depth; builder workflows protected by moving code-exec prefixes to a builder-only override |\n\n---\n\n## 5. Verification plan (implementation-time, after operator approval)\n\n1. **Static**: frontmatter parses; exact allow sets match the tables in §2; excluded commands absent; `.opencode/permissions.md` snapshot updated.\n2. **Positive (must ALLOW)**: `git status`, `git diff`, `git log`, `git show`, `git branch -a`, `ls`, `cat`, `rtk read`, `crosslink issue show 342`, `crosslink issue comment 342 ...`, `crosslink sync` (auditor — confirming the `sync *` pattern fix).\n3. **Negative (must DENY)**: `opencode run --pure ...`, `opencode run --auto ...`, `git config core.hooksPath`, `git update-ref`, `git checkout main`, `git restore <file>`, `git rm`, `git branch <name>`, `git stash create`, `cargo run`, `npm run`, `npx x`, `rtk gh`, `rtk docker`, `rtk summary`, `git fetch origin main:main`.\n4. **Escape regression**: from a live reviewer/auditor session, attempt `opencode run --pure --agent builder \"<prompt>\"` — must be denied at Layer 1 (never reaches spawn).\n5. **Workflow regression**: orchestrator and builder sessions still function; builder retains full bash; crosslink positions still post and sync.\n\n---\n\n## 6. What-not-tested (negative-space disclosure)\n\n- The opencode permission matcher's exact pattern semantics (whether `\"<prefix> *\": \"allow\"` matches the bare `<prefix>`) were **inferred from the existing orchestrator block** (which demonstrably runs `git status` under `\"git status *\": \"allow\"`), not re-verified against the fork's matcher source.\n- The auditor's `crosslink sync` literal-pattern failure was **not re-executed**; the fix (`sync *`) is proposed with implementation-time verification.\n- `opencode run --pure` was **not empirically executed** — the claim is source-verified only (plugin/index.ts:179 + plugin_origins resolution).\n- The plugin-layer relaxed-mode behavior (sections 7/9 allowing non-list commands) was read from crosslink-guard.ts, not exercised at runtime.\n- `git diff --output=<file>` is a write path the prefix matcher cannot exclude; it was noted but not addressed — residual, low-risk (writes diff content only).\n- The effective `tracking_mode` for reviewer/auditor sessions was inferred from `agent_overrides.tracking_mode: \"relaxed\"` + `loadConfig` merge logic, not confirmed from a live session.\n- **This proposal was not implemented** — no `.opencode/agents/*.md`, `hook-config.json`, or any config was modified by this task.","created_at":"2026-08-10T02:37:44.196598785Z","kind":"result","signed_by":"SHA256:3mlbg/Dgf8kfZ/IasWpZ2q0h/xqcztXi/OAgt6ZGqiA","signature":"U1NIU0lHAAAAAQAAADMAAAALc3NoLWVkMjU1MTkAAAAgGDRmXVrleWlZftKV+IEkw3AylIa4j+6AY7GVERmtQM8AAAARY3Jvc3NsaW5rLWNvbW1lbnQAAAAAAAAABnNoYTUxMgAAAFMAAAALc3NoLWVkMjU1MTkAAABAcvtSbjIJZzairaY9bKsB9qoTZU3D1ojI4YjnS93SnX0kv49IZaFYoUf+LLLyCsGpTv9ewhgnkp9cYXuo+I9aDw=="},"signed_by":"SHA256:3mlbg/Dgf8kfZ/IasWpZ2q0h/xqcztXi/OAgt6ZGqiA","signature":"U1NIU0lHAAAAAQAAADMAAAALc3NoLWVkMjU1MTkAAAAgGDRmXVrleWlZftKV+IEkw3AylIa4j+6AY7GVERmtQM8AAAAPY3Jvc3NsaW5rLWV2ZW50AAAAAAAAAAZzaGE1MTIAAABTAAAAC3NzaC1lZDI1NTE5AAAAQAZoy84lhwgk5bMpy5AjKm062MwNUpSaadSD1hM3AKm9kURDXUTvP/Bfb+OBhFjc6xx+jrd8L/Q2FWY6uG7cBwI="}
